# Airport CEO Brief — Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals

**Agent:** airport-ceo (independent Stage 1 research)
**Lens:** long-horizon financial and political positioning — bond covenants, board votes, U&L renegotiation risk, federal-partner alignment
**Date:** 2026-08-07

---

## Key findings a board chair should absorb in one read

1. **The window is measured in months, not years.** Concourse E opens autumn 2026 with roughly 435,000 sq ft of new gate-area program at IAD; the expanded $20–22.5B Dulles program announced in July 2026 will spend the next decade freezing design decisions. A quiet-by-design standard adopted before design manuals are re-issued for that program is an amendment to a document. Adopted after, it is a claim against a hardening capital plan and — depending on how it is written — a change order argument with United, American, and 30-year debt already committed. [Source: MWAA press release, Concourse E opening; Source: White House / DOT joint announcement July 2026 on Dulles expansion]
2. **The credit profile absorbs this easily; the rating narrative benefits.** MWAA sits at Moody's Aa3 / S&P AA- / Fitch AA- (stable) with ~$4.9B outstanding. Rating agencies weight market position, volume, ability to raise rates, coverage, and days cash. A design standard that avoids future retrofit cost, reduces litigation exposure under the DOT ADA rule (49 CFR Part 27), and evidences asset-management discipline reads as credit-neutral to modestly positive under Moody's US Publicly-Managed Airport methodology. [Source: Moody's Investor Service, Publicly Managed Airports and Related Issuers methodology; Source: MWAA investor disclosures]
3. **The 2025 U&L is a shield, not a threat — if we stay inside it.** The 15-year Airport Use Agreement and Premises Lease that took effect January 1, 2025 was adopted alongside a ~$9.0B joint capital plan ($7.0B IAD / $2.39B DCA) with airline sign-off. Anything specified inside that envelope is already funded through rates and charges. Anything that reopens the envelope — or triggers a Majority-in-Interest vote — puts United (IAD) and American (DCA) in the driver's seat on a policy neither dominant carrier asked for. **The design of this standard is downstream of the question "does it fit within the 2025 CIP as adopted, or not?"** [Source: MWAA 2025 Airport Use Agreement announcement; Source: rating-agency reports on 2025 U&L transition]
4. **The federal stack helps at the margin, not at the core.** The FAA Reauthorization Act of 2024 (Pub. L. 118-63, May 16, 2024) sustains AIP formula authority and the Airport Terminal Program (ATP) under BIL/IIJA through FY26 with accessibility, environmental, and passenger-experience selection criteria that align well with a quiet-by-design pitch. But PFC has been frozen at $4.50 since 2001 and no reauthorization moved it. TSA and CBP announcement mandates are federal-partner-controlled and their calendars are not ours. Federal money is a supporting argument, not a foundation. [Source: FAA Reauthorization Act of 2024, Pub. L. 118-63; Source: FAA ATP program guidance; Source: FAA PFC program history]
5. **The board math tolerates one chair transition but not two.** The 17-member board (7 Virginia, 4 D.C., 3 Presidential, 3 Maryland; 6-year terms, one reappointment) turns over roughly 4–5 seats per two-year cycle. A design manual amendment approved in 2026–27 must survive Chair Uncapher's tenure, the next Virginia gubernatorial appointment cycle, and — because the Concourse E-through-C/D replacement arc runs a decade — likely two Presidential appointment rounds. Standards that live inside the Design Manual (staff-adopted, board-noticed) are durable; standards that live in board resolutions are not. [Source: MWAA Board composition and enabling statute, 49 U.S.C. §§ 49101–49112]
6. **The political frame is post-collision, and that cuts both ways.** After the January 29, 2025 midair at DCA, the Washington delegation, the FAA, and airline safety leaders are visibly attentive to communication redundancy. A standard framed as "quiet" reads badly in that room. A standard framed as "intelligibility, redundancy, and accessibility — with fewer, better announcements as a consequence, not a goal" is the only version that survives a Senate Commerce hearing question. Naming matters. [Source: NTSB preliminary reports on DCA midair; Source: MWAA and congressional public statements 2025]
7. **The precedent is one-way.** Once a design standard enters the MWAA Design Manual and is written into consultant scopes for the next Dulles program contract, it becomes the baseline every subsequent airline, tenant, and contractor negotiates against. That is exactly the CEO's leverage — and exactly the reason airlines and concessionaires will push back on the scope in the first thirty days after publication. The fight is worth having once. [Source: airport industry practice; ACRP Report 25 on airport-tenant contract structure]

