## Evidence and analysis

### The bond-rating lens

Moody's *Publicly Managed Airports and Related Issuers* rating methodology assigns weights approximately as follows: Market Position (~35%), Volume Risk (~15%), Ability to Increase Rates and Charges (~15%), Debt Service Coverage (~15%), Days Cash on Hand (~10%), and Debt to Operating Revenues (~10%). MWAA's Aa3 rating sits at the top end of the large-hub cohort, supported by strong demand at both facilities, an investment-grade airline mix, and — critically — a 15-year residual/hybrid U&L that reinforces the "ability to raise rates" factor. [Source: Moody's Investor Service, Publicly Managed Airports and Related Issuers rating methodology]

For a quiet-by-design standard, the rating implications sit in three places:

- **Incremental debt.** If the standard is priced inside the existing 2025 CIP envelope, it is credit-neutral. If it materially increases the CIP, the question is whether the incremental debt increases debt-to-revenue past current thresholds. At current run rates (~$4.9B outstanding, mid-2020s issuance activity) a $50–100M design-standard package is a rounding error. A $500M program is a rating-committee conversation. [Source: MWAA Series 2025 Official Statement; Source: MWAA outstanding-debt disclosures]
- **Days cash on hand.** MWAA's liquidity is disclosed in its Series official statements and CAFRs. Rating agencies look at trend, not level. A design standard that draws on cash reserves during a capital-heavy decade is more sensitive than one that draws on par debt. Fund the standard from bond proceeds, not liquidity. [Source: MWAA Comprehensive Annual Financial Reports]
- **Concentration risk narrative.** United is ~70%+ of IAD, American is the dominant carrier at DCA. Any policy that United or American openly opposes gives rating agencies a "governance" data point. Any policy that arrives with airline consent — even reluctant consent — reinforces the "collaborative U&L" narrative that S&P and Fitch have both cited in recent MWAA reports. [Source: DOT T-100 domestic segment data; Source: S&P Global Ratings MWAA reports]

### Airline use-and-lease structure

The 2025 U&L is the operative document. Three CEO-relevant features:

- **It is a 15-year commitment.** The next full renegotiation window is calendar 2039. Interim amendments require airline agreement. That is a very long runway to write a standard *into* the design manual rather than into the U&L. [Source: MWAA 2025 U&L announcement]
- **MII provisions govern which capital projects airlines can veto.** The specific thresholds and project categories in the 2025 U&L were not directly verified in the context pass and must be confirmed against the executed document; industry convention would suggest thresholds tied to project dollar value, budgeted rate impact, and whether the project is inside the pre-approved CIP. A quiet-by-design standard that (a) sits inside the pre-approved CIP and (b) does not trigger the MII rate-impact threshold does not need airline sign-off. That is the design objective. [Source: ACRP Report 36, Airport/Airline Agreements — Practices and Characteristics; Source: MWAA executed 2025 U&L, to be verified]
- **The dominant-carrier asymmetry is different at each airport.** United's IAD hub is now inside a joint expansion announcement with the White House and DOT — a political posture in which United is unlikely to fight small design details but very likely to fight anything that slips schedule on Concourse E or the C/D replacement. American at DCA, by contrast, is operating in a post-collision environment with heightened scrutiny of anything touching communication. The same standard reads differently to the two carriers. Expect asymmetric response. [Source: July 2026 joint expansion announcement; Source: American Airlines and DCA post-collision public statements 2025]

### The federal stack

- **FAA Reauthorization.** Pub. L. 118-63 (May 16, 2024) is the operative statute through FY28 for AIP formula authority and program structure. It extended DCA's beyond-perimeter slot exemptions and left the PFC cap at $4.50 (unchanged since 2001). Nothing in the reauthorization directly funds quiet-by-design standards, but the ATP selection criteria (accessibility, passenger experience, environmental) are usable in future ATP applications. [Source: Pub. L. 118-63, congress.gov; Source: FAA Airport Terminal Program NOFO 2024–2026]
- **AIP eligibility.** AIP eligible categories are narrow. Acoustic treatment inside a terminal is generally not AIP-eligible; airfield noise (Part 150) is a different program. Do not confuse the two in a board briefing. [Source: FAA Order 5100.38, AIP Handbook]
- **TSA and CBP.** TSA's public address announcement program has been relaxed post-2019 to permit reductions and localization at operator discretion, but current specific guidance was not verified in the context pass. CBP intelligibility and signage requirements apply inside FIS halls at IAD and are non-negotiable. A quiet-by-design standard must carve out the sterile international arrivals path or run into CBP head-on. [Source: TSA and CBP announcement guidance, to be verified]
- **BIL/IIJA sunset.** The Bipartisan Infrastructure Law's ATP formula funding runs through FY26. Any standard whose funding case depends on continued ATP appropriation must acknowledge the reauthorization risk in the FY27+ FAA reauthorization cycle. [Source: Bipartisan Infrastructure Law, Pub. L. 117-58 §§ 25001–25004]

### Board governance

MWAA is a public-body corporate created under the Metropolitan Washington Airports Act of 1986 (49 U.S.C. §§ 49101–49112). Its 17-member board is appointed by four different authorities on 6-year staggered terms with a single reappointment permitted. Two governance features matter for a design standard:

- **The Design Manual is staff-adopted.** Historically at MWAA, engineering standards enter through the Design Manual under the President/CEO's delegated authority. Board approval is required only when rates, charges, U&L obligations, or CIP totals materially change. This is the correct vehicle for a quiet-by-design standard. A board resolution vehicle would elevate a technical standard to a political fight, and every appointment cycle would revisit it. [Source: MWAA enabling statute; Source: MWAA delegation-of-authority policy]
- **Compared to a city department (Miami-Dade), an airport district (Metropolitan Nashville Airport Authority), or a port authority (PANYNJ), MWAA's independent-authority structure is unusually insulated from local political turnover.** That is a strategic advantage in decade-long capital planning and a reason to write standards that presume institutional continuity. [Source: GAO 15-42, Airport Funding and Development; Source: ACRP Synthesis 43, Airport Governance]

### The host region

- **Fairfax and Loudoun County (IAD).** Both counties are direct commercial beneficiaries of Dulles expansion. Neither will oppose a design standard. The relevant political actor at IAD is the Virginia delegation, which sees IAD as an economic engine and United's Dulles hub as a state-industrial asset. [Source: Fairfax County Economic Development Authority statements; Source: Virginia congressional delegation press releases on 2026 Dulles announcement]
- **Arlington County (DCA).** The post-collision political environment at DCA is more sensitive. Arlington County and the D.C. Council have raised community concerns about DCA operations that overlap with communication and safety. A standard that appears to reduce announcements at DCA without a strong redundancy and safety narrative attracts organized local opposition. [Source: Arlington County Board statements 2025; Source: D.C. Council transportation committee hearings 2025]
- **Business community.** The Greater Washington Partnership and the airline-served business community are generally aligned with MWAA on capital investment. A quiet-by-design standard framed as passenger-experience upgrade lands well with this constituency. Framed as an amenity, it lands badly. [Source: Greater Washington Partnership annual reports]

### The CEO's calendar as evidence

Public statements and interviews from large-hub CEOs (LAWA, Massport, Port of Seattle, PANYNJ, DFW, Miami-Dade) in ACI-NA and AAAE forums 2023–2025 consistently show the following calendar allocation: capital program governance (~30–35%), government affairs and federal partner relations (~20–25%), airline and tenant relations (~15–20%), board and community relations (~15%), operations exception management (~10%). Very little time is spent on standards documents — which is precisely why standards documents are undervalued as strategic instruments. A CEO who spends 20 hours over 90 days sponsoring a design standard through the design manual buys a decade of downstream design leverage at a marginal cost of their own time. [Source: ACI-NA conference programs 2023–2025; Source: peer CEO interviews in Airport Business, Airport World, AAAE Airport magazine]

### Long-tenure strategic patterns 2000–2025

Three patterns that look obvious in hindsight:

- **Get the design standard in before the money moves.** LAX's automated people mover began construction in 2018 for a planned 2023 opening; it will open in 2026 at multi-billion-dollar cost with substantial operational rework because the interface standards between LAWA, tenants, and airlines were not fully specified before design hardened. [Source: LAWA APM program reports; Source: LA Metro/LAWA joint updates]
- **Do not confuse airline agreement with airline enthusiasm.** DEN's automated baggage system (1994–95) was airline-specified and airline-endorsed until it wasn't; the airport ate the cost. Airline consent that follows a standard is durable; airline consent that produces a standard is fragile. [Source: DIA baggage system after-action reports; GAO/RCED-95-35BR]
- **Governance vehicles outlive their sponsors.** JFK's redevelopment stalled for two decades under PANYNJ direct-build models and only unlocked when the private-terminal model (T1, T4, T6) restructured the incentives. The lesson: the vehicle that adopts a standard matters more than the standard itself. Put quiet-by-design into a Design Manual that survives, not a memo that doesn't. [Source: PANYNJ JFK Vision Plan 2017; Source: JFK Millennium Partners / New Terminal One press materials 2022–2025]

Two that looked obvious at the time and turned out wrong:

- **Common-use everywhere.** Many U.S. hubs pushed common-use as a universal standard 2005–2015. Where hub-carrier operations dominated, it created integration friction with the dominant carrier's operating systems and was quietly rolled back. Lesson for MWAA: distinguish rules that apply universally (IAD Concourse E and DCA Terminal 1 alike) from rules that a hub carrier will always work around. [Source: SITA and ARINC common-use deployment reports 2010–2020]
- **Under-specifying accessibility.** Terminals designed 2005–2015 that met minimum ADA at the time now face 2020s DOJ and DOT enforcement actions over effective communication, sensory accessibility, and neurodivergent passenger accommodations. The cost of the retrofits exceeds the cost of doing it right in original design by an order of magnitude. This is the strongest single argument for quiet-by-design as a *design* standard, not an operations initiative. [Source: DOJ ADA enforcement actions in transportation 2020–2025; Source: DOT NPRM on airport accessibility 2024]

