## The financial-and-political case, as the CEO sees it

**For the thesis:**
- The Concourse E opening and the $20–22.5B expanded Dulles program create the single largest design-decision surface MWAA has faced in a generation. Standards adopted now govern a decade of construction. Standards adopted later govern nothing.
- The 2025 U&L already funded the CIP envelope. A standard priced inside that envelope is airline-neutral. That is the specific space to occupy.
- The rating story is helped, not hurt, by evidence of long-horizon asset management discipline.
- DOT ADA enforcement and DOJ effective-communication trends are moving; ahead-of-the-curve compliance is cheaper as design than as retrofit.
- The Design Manual is a durable governance vehicle that outlives chair transitions.

**Against the thesis:**
- "Quiet" is the wrong word in a post-collision political environment. If the standard cannot be renamed and reframed around intelligibility, redundancy, and accessibility, it should not be adopted.
- Any standard that reopens the CIP envelope hands airline signatories — and specifically United at IAD — a lever they did not previously have. That is a bad trade regardless of the merits of the standard.
- Historic preservation at IAD Main Terminal and DCA Terminal 1 constrains retrofit scope; the standard must be honest about the two airports being different implementations of the same principles, not a single prescription.
- TSA and CBP federal-partner mandates operate on calendars MWAA does not control. Overpromising on announcement reduction risks a public embarrassment when a federal partner declines to move.
- Every design standard has a maintenance cost: commissioning, verification, and post-opening measurement. That is a recurring O&M charge, and the airlines will see it in rates and charges. Under-scope this and the standard becomes shelfware within one turnover cycle.

**Net CEO position.** The thesis is worth pursuing, but the pursuit is a governance and framing exercise before it is a technical exercise. The right vehicle is a Design Manual amendment adopted under delegated authority, sequenced ahead of the Concourse E lessons-learned close-out and ahead of consultant scoping for the C/D replacement package. The right frame is intelligibility, redundancy, and accessibility — not silence. The right funding posture is inside the 2025 CIP envelope. If any of those three conditions cannot be met, the standard is not ready to adopt.

