## Data points and precedents a strategist can use

1. **MWAA credit position.** Aa3 / AA- / AA-, stable outlook, ~$4.9B outstanding as of mid-2025 disclosures; 2036 AMT maturity from June 2025 issuance yielded ~4.37%. Baseline for any capital-cost sensitivity work. [Source: MWAA Series 2025 Official Statement; Source: rating agency reports]
2. **U&L capital envelope.** $9.0B joint capital plan ($7.0B IAD + $2.39B DCA) inside the 2025 15-year U&L; expansion to $20–22.5B announced July 2026 with funding structure publicly unresolved. Any standard priced above these envelopes triggers a different conversation. [Source: MWAA 2025 U&L; Source: July 2026 White House/DOT/United/MWAA joint announcement]
3. **PFC cap frozen at $4.50 since 2001.** Two decades of aviation cost inflation have compressed PFC purchasing power roughly 40%. Any argument that assumes PFC increases is not a serious argument. [Source: FAA PFC program history; Bureau of Labor Statistics CPI-U]
4. **NFPA 72 STI ≥ 0.50 requirement** governs emergency voice/alarm communications intelligibility in U.S. terminals via building-code adoption; this is the existing regulatory floor that any MWAA standard must exceed to be meaningful. [Source: NFPA 72 National Fire Alarm and Signaling Code, current edition]
5. **DEN baggage system and JFK PANYNJ governance stall** as precedents for airline consent that turned adversarial (DEN) and governance vehicles that constrained a two-decade capital cycle (JFK) — both cited in academic case studies and GAO products, and both directly relevant to the "how does this standard survive?" question at MWAA. [Source: GAO/RCED-95-35BR; Source: PANYNJ Vision Plan 2017 and successor documents]

